      correctAnswer: "A",
      explanation: "**Choice A is correct.** The sentence is turned around, and the thing doing the being is the plural \"several fossilized tree stumps,\" so the verb must be the plural \"are.\"\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- First ask: what is this sentence actually about? Flip it back into normal order - \"Several fossilized tree stumps ... are among the specimens now on display.\"\n- Once flipped, the subject is plainly the plural \"stumps,\" not the singular \"hall\" that happens to sit right before the blank.\n- \"Now on display\" keeps the sentence in the present, so the plural present verb \"are\" is the match.\n- How to spot it: when a sentence opens with a phrase like \"Among the ...,\" the subject comes AFTER the verb; find it before choosing.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- B: \"Is\" agrees with the nearby \"hall\" rather than with the real subject, \"stumps.\"\n- C: \"Has been\" is also singular, and it shifts the time frame away from the present the sentence sets.\n- D: \"Being\" is not a working verb and would leave the sentence without one.",
      passage: "Among the specimens now on display in the museum's paleobotany hall ______ several fossilized tree stumps from a Devonian forest, their broad root systems still spreading outward through the surrounding rock much as they spread through soil hundreds of millions of years ago.",
